<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thuwaini bin Said</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-bu-said-dynasty">Al Bu Said dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thuwaini bin Said was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> from 1856 to 1866. The eldest surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-sultan">Said ibn Sultan</a>, he inherited the Arabian lands while his brother Majid took Zanzibar; a planned expedition against Zanzibar was halted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canning-award">Canning Award</a> of 1861 confirmed the division. He was killed in 1866 by his son Salim.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thuwaini-bin-said.jpg" alt="Thuwaini bin Said" width="175" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thuwaini_bin_Said.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Said died at sea in 1856, his sons divided his realm: <b>Thuwaini</b> took Muscat and Oman, while Majid bin Said became sultan of Zanzibar and the African dominions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When Said ibn Sultan of the Omani Empire died in 1856, his son <b>Thuwaini</b> took Muscat and another son Majid took Zanzibar.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman">Sultanate of Muscat and Oman</a></li></ul></section>
